---
import StudyHeader from '../../../components/StudyHeader.astro';
import StudyEvidence from '../../../components/StudyEvidence.astro';
import { studies } from '../../../data/studies';
import Site from '../../../layouts/Site.astro';
import EnergyExplorer from '../../../components/EnergyExplorer';
import EnergyStudyBrief from '../../../components/EnergyStudyBrief.astro';
import index from '../../../data/energy-index.json';
import sensitivity from '../../../data/energy-sensitivity.json';
---
<Site title="Can electricity storage reduce daily peaks?" description="An open-data study of electricity storage scheduling: the decisions, forecasts and physical constraints behind peak reduction using Great Britain’s demand data.">
<StudyHeader study={studies.energy} />
<EnergyStudyBrief />
<section class="tint experiment-section" id="explorer"><div class="shell">
  <div class="explorer-intro">
    <h2>Try the storage scenarios</h2>
    <p>Choose a store’s power and duration, then compare schedules for a day. These are evaluated scenarios: the model chooses the half-hour charging and discharging flows for each size. <a href="#model-brief-heading">Review the model and assumptions ↑</a></p>
    <p><strong>Read the result as a change in the daily peak.</strong> Peak reduction is the highest observed requirement minus the highest requirement after adding the storage schedule. A forecast error can place charging at the wrong time and create a higher peak, giving a negative result.</p>
  </div>
  <EnergyExplorer index={index} client:load />
  <noscript><p>The default evaluated scenario is shown above. Enable JavaScript to change the date or capacity, or inspect complete results in the Colab notebook below.</p></noscript>
  <p class="source-credit">Supported by <a href="https://www.neso.energy/data-portal/historic-demand-data">National Energy SO Open Data</a>. Historic Demand Data 2024/2025 · <a href="https://www.neso.energy/data-portal/neso-open-licence">NESO Open Data Licence</a>. Processing and model: Abdulaziz Aldoseri.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="section shell study-prose" id="findings">
  <p class="eyebrow">The decision implication</p>
  <h2>How the schedule is chosen<br /><span class="serif">matters.</span></h2>
  <div class="finding-grid">
    <p class="large-copy">For the fixed solver selection shown above, 1,000 MW and two hours of storage provide <strong>532.1 MW</strong> of average daily peak relief, compared with <strong>514.4 MW</strong> for the rule. That selected schedule raises the actual peak on <strong>15 of 245 days</strong>; the rule does so on none in this test period.</p>
    <div><p>The forecast objective does not uniquely determine the operating schedule. Different schedules can achieve the same optimal forecast peak and minimum throughput, yet behave differently when actual demand arrives.</p><p>The perfect-information bound averages 964.1 MW of relief. It describes ideal potential. A complete operating policy must also specify how it chooses among equally good forecast solutions.</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="scope-note" id="tie-sensitivity">
    <p class="eyebrow">Sensitivity check · exploratory</p>
    <h3>The same forecast objectives can lead to different outcomes.</h3>
    <p>An adversarial review added a third objective: favour holding energy for longer, while preserving the original optimal forecast peak and minimum throughput. At 1,000 MW/two hours, this alternative selection averaged <strong>{sensitivity.time_based_tie_variants.max_holding_time.mean_mw.toFixed(1)} MW</strong> of relief and raised the peak on <strong>{sensitivity.time_based_tie_variants.max_holding_time.worsened_days} days</strong>. No actual 2025 demand entered that third objective.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Schedule selection sensitivity"><table><caption>1,000 MW / two hours · same 245 days · sensitivity examined after the primary evaluation</caption><thead><tr><th>Schedule selection</th><th>Mean relief (MW)</th><th>Days worsened</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Frozen forecast LP selection</th><td>532.1</td><td>15 / 245</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Longer holding time, same two objective levels</th><td>{sensitivity.time_based_tie_variants.max_holding_time.mean_mw.toFixed(1)}</td><td>0 / 245</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Forecast-window rule</th><td>514.4</td><td>0 / 245</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
    <p><strong>This is a post-hoc diagnostic, not a newly validated policy.</strong> It was investigated after inspecting the original evaluation. Selecting the alternative now and presenting the same days as a fresh test would overstate the evidence. The original interactive schedules remain unchanged.</p>
    <p>The next step is to define the full schedule-selection rule and evaluate it on fresh data. The diagnostic code, day-level comparison and objective checks are included in the reproduction package.</p>
  </div>
  <p class="scope-note">These are retrospective model results for hypothetical additional capacity, not measured benefits from a deployed storage asset or a recommendation for a real operating system.</p>
</section>
<section class="section tint" id="method"><div class="shell study-prose">
  <p class="eyebrow">Evaluation &amp; reproducibility</p>
  <h2>How the results were checked</h2>
  <div class="method-grid">
    <article><span class="number">01</span><h3>A later test period</h3><p>During development, the first 21 days of each 2024 month fit the profile and the remaining 114 days validate it. The final profile fits all of 2024. Evaluation then covers 1 May–31 December 2025: 245 days.</p></article>
    <article><span class="number">02</span><h3>Keep the adverse days</h3><p>Each day has equal weight in the period average. Negative peak reductions stay in the results. A day counts as worsened when the increase exceeds 0.0001 MW, allowing for numerical noise. Peak reduction in MW is neither energy saved nor money saved.</p></article>
    <article><span class="number">03</span><h3>Verify every schedule</h3><p>The Python model uses SciPy/HiGHS. Analytic examples, an independent reachability check, information-leakage checks and reconciliation of exported schedules support numerical verification. Clock-change days retain their 46 or 50 real half-hours.</p></article>
  </div>
  <p class="scope-note">The annual source files are revised snapshots. This is a chronological retrospective demonstration, not an as-issued operational backtest. Original schedules and results remain frozen; the later schedule-selection sensitivity is identified separately above.</p>
</div></section>
